  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>This 4-star campsite in Le Bar-sur-Loup covers 165 ares, at the entrance to the Gorges du Loup, in the Alpes-Maritimes. It offers an open view of the surroundings, among dozens of species of trees (sequoias, centuries-old olive trees, oaks, eucalyptus) and fruit trees. It has 70 pitches, mobile homes, chalets and gîtotels, a heated swimming pool, a snack bar and a play area.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The 2026 price list published on the campsite's website has a "dog on a leash with certificate" line at €3.60, identical in low season (March to June, September-October) and high season (July-August). The campsite's guide specifies: dog accepted on a leash, with a vaccination certificate, €3.60 per day. For comparison, a pitch for two people with a vehicle costs €19 in low season and €28 in high season, an extra person €7.</p>
      <p>The campsite is open in season, with no single date: the sources consulted give different periods, and the site showed "currently closed" when read. Ask for the exact dates of your stay before booking. The rental accommodations are not accessible to people with reduced mobility; for pitches, the sloping topography means you should choose with the reception.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Gorges du Loup and the hilltop village of Le Bar-sur-Loup are right nearby, for a walk with your dog between two swims.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The medieval village of Le Bar-sur-Loup can be reached on foot in under a kilometre, according to the campsite's ADAC and ANWB listings. The campsite's website also mentions the Gorges du Loup (canyoning from age 8, with a local partner), Gourdon, Tourrettes-sur-Loup, Grasse, Antibes and Marineland; it does not say which outings are suitable for dogs.</p>
